Key findings
SLoTH beats or matches PINet on 9/10 problem-dimension pairs while being 2-35× faster
The only accuracy loss is QP at d=150 (2.7× worse), where SLoTH is still 15× faster
β = 0.9 works universally — only α and k need tuning per problem type
Per-sample warm-starting is essential — amortizes convergence across training epochs
The step_final^T projection of ∇_z L into s-space is critical for correct adjoint RHS
At high dimensions (d=2000), SLoTH has a narrow stability window for α but within that window dramatically outperforms PINet on SOCP (270× better accuracy)
SLoTH-CA annealing improves MPC by allowing aggressive early steps (α=0.8) without late-stage divergence
SLoTH-RM gives the best SOCP results (4.8e-09 at d=100, 1.1e-06 at d=500) via variance-reduced gradient tracking
SLoTH-CARM (cosine + recursive momentum) was tested on MPC but did not beat HB or CA — MPC's fast-changing gradients break RM's variance-reduction assumption
SLoTH-HB and SLoTH-CA are the recommended MPC variants; SLoTH-RM is recommended for SOCP
All four variants dramatically outperform PINet on SOCP across all dimensions
